decisions:
  - id: swiftterm-production
    decision: SwiftTerm validated for production use in Conductor
    rationale: Sprint 0 spike proved Claude Code v2.1.86 renders correctly. Jinx's concerns (OSC 133, performance, rendering) were not observed. No fallback to xterm.js needed.
  - id: conductor-workspace-mode
    decision: Conductor is now a workspace application, not just an overlay
    rationale: Embedded terminals + sidebar + Symphony auto-link + keyboard shortcuts = full AI development workspace. Overlay mode preserved as alternative for lightweight monitoring.
  - id: nsview-focus-detection
    decision: Use NSEvent.addLocalMonitorForEvents for terminal focus detection
    rationale: SwiftTerm's NSView eats mouse events. Cannot override mouseDown (non-open). Cannot use SwiftUI onTapGesture. Local event monitor with bounds check is the only reliable approach.
  - id: observedobject-for-live-ui
    decision: TerminalCellView uses @ObservedObject on TerminalSessionManager for immediate focus ring updates
    rationale: Static let values and let _ tricks don't trigger SwiftUI redraw in ForEach with static ranges. Direct @ObservedObject subscription is the correct pattern.
learnings:
  - SwiftTerm handles Claude Code's full Ink TUI without issues — colors, ASCII art, interactive prompt, streaming output, status bar all render correctly
  - NSEvent.addLocalMonitorForEvents is the correct pattern when an NSView subclass eats mouse events and you cannot override (non-open methods)
  - '@ObservedObject on child views is the correct SwiftUI pattern when ForEach with static ranges needs to react to external state changes'
  - Conductor .app bundle with ad-hoc signing retains accessibility permissions across rebuilds when using a stable CFBundleIdentifier
  - The entire workspace evolution (4 sprints) was completed in one session — empirical spikes settle architectural debates faster than theoretical discussion
